NOTICE — provenance and prior art
=================================

vetted-skills (`vsk`) is original code, MIT licensed, (c) 2026 oxbfnohom.

No source code was copied, adapted, or vendored from another project. This file records
what was *read* while building it, so the boundary is checkable rather than claimed.

What was consulted, and what was taken from each
------------------------------------------------

1. skills.sh / vercel-labs/skills (registry + CLI)
   Taken: the HTTP shape of the public search API only — the endpoint path
   (`GET https://skills.sh/api/search?q=…`) and the two response fields actually used
   (`skills[].id`, `skills[].installs`). Discoverable in one `curl`, and documented by use.
   Not taken: any CLI code, output formatting, archive handling, agent-detection logic,
   telemetry, or diagnostics.

2. NVIDIA/SkillSpector (scanner)
   Taken: nothing at code level. Used as an *optional external scanner* through its public
   CLI, and its reports (which flag unpinned `npx` in install docs as a medium issue) are
   cited in the README as prior art for the verdict model.
   Not taken: its rule set, scoring, or report schema — `vsk` only needs a generic JSON
   pass (`verdict` / `score` / `findings`) to merge a foreign verdict.

3. Agent runtimes with a built-in skills hub
   Taken: the *shape* of a trust model (builtin / trusted / community / agent-created, and
   the rule that a caution verdict on a community source is blocked). This is a design
   idea about policies, not code; `vsk` implements its own thresholds and its own
   verdict decisions in `src/vsk/rules.py`.

4. Other scanners surveyed for the field map (Tencent AI-Infra-Guard, snyk/agent-scan,
   cisco-ai-defense/skill-scanner, safeskill, skillvet, skilld, and several small
   "vet before install" repositories)
   Taken: nothing. They are listed in `docs/prior-art.md` so users can compare.

Rule set
--------

Every rule in `src/vsk/rules.py` was written from the threat pattern itself (what a
download-piped-into-shell, a credential read, a webhook POST, or a persistence write looks
like in text). Rule ids, categories, severities and thresholds are ours.

Public endpoints used at runtime
--------------------------------

* `https://skills.sh/api/search`  — registry search (`vsk search`)
* `https://api.github.com/...`    — repo metadata, ref resolution (`vsk vet`/`install`)
* `https://codeload.github.com/...` — repo tarball, from which only the skill folder is read

All three are public HTTP interfaces addressed by `urllib` from the standard library.
No third-party package is required to run `vsk`.
